  "body": "## Issue\n\nWith respect to the pattern implementation at: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#e-mail-state-(type=email)\n\nThe issue comes from the assumption that the '@' symbol is the default character at `U+0040`. However, this causes issues when other variants (`U+FF20, U+FE6B`) are used. An example of this case happening is if a user enters an email address using a Japanese keyboard input method, which may use the FULL-WIDTH COMMERCIAL variant, and then fail validation.\n\nThe example case above came from implementing client-side form validation using the jquery-validation plugin, which references this pattern for implementation:\nhttps://github.com/jzaefferer/jquery-validation/blob/master/src/core.js#L1321\n## Potential solution\n\nSwapping the `@` to `[@\\uFF20\\uFE6B]` resolves this pattern issue, at least for JavaScript, I'm not familiar enough with perl and can't speak to whether it resolves the input issue there.\n\n> /^[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&amp;'*+\\/=​?^_`{|}~-]+@​[a-zA-Z0-9](?:​[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:​\\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:​[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?)*$/\n",
